From 25853ce809726b87324dc27ff4c0fe2bafb00687 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 21:50:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: sun6i-isp: Add a statistics node

The hardware measures exposure, focus, flicker, white balance and a
luminance histogram into a buffer it fills once per frame. The buffer
was already allocated and its address already programmed; nothing read
it back.

Add a metadata capture device for it, modelled on rkisp1: a bitmask of
which blocks are valid for the frame, followed by one structure per
block. Which blocks ran is read back out of the module enable register
rather than tracked separately, and the ones that did not are left
zeroed.

The structures are the hardware layouts rather than a repacking, so each
block is a straight copy of its region and the sizes come out equal to
the regions the vendor headers describe. The copy happens on the frame
finish interrupt, which is when the hardware is done writing.

The region at 0x800 is left out. Nothing in the vendor driver reads it
and there is no evidence for what the hardware puts there.

These definitions are tentative and marked as such in the header. The
record sizes and counts come from the vendor parsers -- strides from
pointer arithmetic, counts from loop bounds -- so those should be right.
What is inferred rather than shown is which sum is which colour, taken
from the green-dominant weighting the vendor applies. The exposure block
carries a further caveat: the vendor points a second plane at zone 64
and a third past the end of the region entirely, which does not
reconcile with the region being the size it is, so the whole region is
handed over and only the first 64 zones should be trusted.

This is meant to be run against known input and corrected.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
